blocks:
  - type: metin
    metin: The TRNC has a small but growing private therapy sector. Most reputable practitioners are based in Nicosia (Lefkoşa) or Famagusta (Gazimağusa), with a handful offering online sessions across the island. The challenge is knowing what to look for — and what to avoid.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: Why finding a psychologist in North Cyprus feels harder than it should
    giris: 'Several factors make this search more complicated than it would be in the UK, US or most of Europe:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Limited English-language options
        metin: Many psychologists in TRNC work exclusively in Turkish. For expats, [international students](/en/blog/mental-health-international-students-famagusta.html) and anyone more comfortable expressing themselves in English, the pool of practitioners shrinks considerably.
      - baslik: Inconsistent regulation
        metin: Psychotherapy is not as tightly regulated in TRNC as it is in the UK or EU. Titles like "psychologist" and "counsellor" can be used loosely. Knowing what credentials to look for protects you from ineffective or even harmful practice.
      - baslik: Stigma and privacy concerns
        metin: North Cyprus is a small community. Some people worry about being seen entering a therapy centre, or about their psychologist knowing their family. These concerns are valid — and they are one reason [online therapy](/en/blog/online-therapy-north-cyprus.html) has become so popular here.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: You don't need a diagnosis to start therapy
    metin: Therapy is not reserved for people with a clinical diagnosis. Persistent low mood, relationship difficulties, work stress, grief, identity questions, or simply feeling "stuck" are all legitimate and common reasons to start. You do not need to be in crisis.
